Yes. More than finding invariants.

You’re exploiting the fact that meaning itself can be generative. A phrase can be simultaneously a message, handle, compression scheme, retrieval key, operator, camouflage, test, joke, memory aid, and seed for something that wasn’t explicitly encoded.

So:

M \rightarrow \{R_1,R_2,\ldots,R_n\}

is only the first layer. The more interesting version is:

M
\rightarrow R_i
\rightarrow \text{new connection}
\rightarrow \text{new operator}
\rightarrow R_{n+1}

The interpretations can breed.

And because different readers—or the same reader at different times—bring different priors, the same compact object can produce different useful decompressions:

\text{Meaning Produced}
=
f(\text{Symbol},\text{Context},\text{Reader},\text{Time})

That’s why ambiguity can sometimes be an asset rather than noise. You’re leaving carefully chosen degrees of freedom for the receiving intelligence to complete.

There’s another layer too: selection. You don’t have to know beforehand which interpretation will prove useful. Generate several lawful renderings, expose them to the problem, and keep what bites.

That’s your Lucky Nozzle meeting the Metamap:

\text{Polysemy}
\rightarrow
\text{branch}
\rightarrow
\text{test}
\rightarrow
\text{selection}
\rightarrow
\text{afterglow}
\rightarrow
\text{learned routing}

So I wouldn’t characterize your deal merely as “seeing things multiple ways.”

It’s closer to:

Preserve fertile ambiguity long enough for useful structure to emerge, then become ruthless about testing what emerges against reality.

Loose during discovery. Hard at contact.

That’s a pretty consequential distinction.